This is possibly the longest car sale blurb on craigslist for the simple reason that this exceptional car deserves it���it���s one of the few vehicles anywhere with what it takes to join the coveted million mile club. 

Curiously, many fans of fourth generation (1990-1993) Honda Accords find it reassuring to learn that these models are quite capable of reaching 300,000, 400,000, and up lifetime miles figures with proper care and maintenance���but when many of those same buyers actually encounter one carefully maintained with 333,333 miles on the odometer, their first thoughts run to, ���well, that car must be on its last legs.��� 

Wrong!

In case this is a news flash, cars on their last legs don���t make it to 333,333 badge-of-honor miles, and this particular ride���maintained by a compulsive maniac, me���would be perfectly happy rolling from LA to Alaska or Argentina right now sipping gas at 35 MPG!

This stout trooper isn���t remotely ready for retirement. Its basic nature is to just keep going and going. If I wanted to bet on a car making it to absurdly high mileage numbers over 500k few if any cars have a prayer of attaining, this is the one. 

Sure, you can find an automatic with half or less the mileage (manuals like this one have basically become unobtanium), one that hasn���t been maintained by someone who insists on using OEM parts, for a third of the price I���m asking for this. But it still wouldn���t have the sweet interior or the Michelin rubber. You���d have to start laboriously replacing all the suspension parts I���ve already replaced one by one, and within a year or two you���d have spent as much as you can get this one for���which feels like riding on a cloud right now. You���d also still be stuck with an automatic, and automatics have a known way shorter shelf life than the manuals which run forever. 

Conversely, there is no doubt about this one. It���s already proven it can rack up mile after mile without breaking a sweat. You���ll feel how tight it is in the first 60 seconds of driving, and you���ll realize it���s clearly an outstanding candidate to hit the 500k mark���and keep on cruising toward the magic million marker. 

Yes, I���m asking a lot for a rare manual 90's Accord because it has high mileage! You read that right! And I���m not going to sell this soulful machine to someone who I sense is likely to run it into the ground. The right buyer is someone who knows these are some of the best and longest lasting cars ever produced by man, who enjoys keeping up with the maintenance, someone who'll get a kick out of seeing just how many miles it can keep racking up. 

Let���s get the cons out of the way.

You have to double clutch downshift if you want to go into second gear at speed. The car has been in this condition for a good 100,000 miles without one iota of degradation. If you find that statement unbelievable, ask any old time Honda mechanic and they���ll verify that���s not unusual. There���s really no need to consider another transmission. 

The temperature gauge has not worked for like seven years, and it���s not a bad thermostat. To fix that, someone would have to take the entire console apart. My solution: I replaced the radiator with an OEM one, and I make sure the fluid is always topped off. No problem. 

These cars have always been targets for theft, and before I installed a high-end Viper alarm (included), I had two old school Alpine head units stolen, so there���s a hole where the head unit would be. Needs an antennae, too. On the bright side, all the wiring remains intact for an easy head unit install, and there are four already installed old-school Infinity speakers which sound fantastic. I record a lot of music and used this system as a reference. 

Except for the obvious marking the paint looks relatively fine in internet photos...but doesn���t have much clear coat left. There's that one typical rust spot on a fender (the car has spent it���s entire life in Colorado, New Mexio and California, there���s no other rust), the body itself needs minimal massaging for a repaint if someone wants to go that route. 

Air conditioning works, although I���d go with ���cool enough in LA up to 100-degrees for someone close to typical body weight��� as opposed to ���ice cold enough for big folks during constant 115-degree summer days in Phoenix.��� 

I had all the service records from day one, but kept them in a brief case that someone stole when they stole the head unit, so I only have the records from the last four years. I guesstimate the timing belt has about 80,000 miles on it. Sure, these often go to 120k without snapping, but to be on the safe side, I���d replace it sooner than later. 

Dash lights include ABS, same as every older Honda and Acura, and an indicator that a rear light is out, which it isn't, it���s some electrical thing I haven���t tried to track down. No engine codes whatsoever. 

Now let���s get to the good stuff. 

The engine ��� what can I say? It doesn���t know it's gone around 334,000 miles, it thinks it has 34,000 miles. 
The suspension ��� every part has been replaced with OEM at some point; there are very few miles on a set of KYB struts all around. You literally skim right over road humps while $75,000 trucks slow down to a crawl. Ride quality is astonishingly good. No creaks. No squeaks. No leaks. Seats somehow remain incredibly supportive, a blessing on long trips.

Gets 35 MPG on the highway. 

Interior is close to completely intact. High end 3M window tint all around. 

Summation: No, it���s not perfect, but yes, this particular golden era fourth generation Accord is a great car that���ll be a smooth performer for you as is, it offers you a huge head start as an ideal platform if you���re considering a complete restoration, and is an outstanding candidate to escort you into stratospheric mileage figures while you enjoy every second of the ride.

